  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be irreflexive, we require \n\n$$\n(1,1)\\notin R,\\,(2,2)\\notin R,\\,(3,3)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268138/assets/b63d99459e8271e70ec36fd417d67565f3d5e77316815d49c235468e174aa1e9.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to an irreflexive relation must not contain diagonal points. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
